#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac86d18-0608-5583-b9c7-c7f6edf37c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177365} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dcd06e-fb31-5a97-8d6c-6bc74d4db44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177366} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7279f3-67ed-5139-aebe-5d7d64a44fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177367} {:answer/id #uuid "3b337070-2da3-5a92-a635-740256268107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177368} {:answer/id #uuid "3efdd6d2-cd5f-5e8a-ad19-19f5db0751fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b337070-2da3-5a92-a635-740256268107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c9c876-c3a8-5b4d-84ca-cbb8b8293da8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc5209a4-b61b-5cd1-9218-3472dc4617b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335007} {:answer/id #uuid "a03b89c4-aab5-5ce8-8812-718451bed2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335008} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a044ea-a7c1-518d-8bb1-c7d101d16c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335009} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a848f8-177d-5d39-bb81-f237398265eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335010} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa5fb1e-9eb6-599b-bc54-eb4d9ed67161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5209a4-b61b-5cd1-9218-3472dc4617b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335007} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49236b6-51a8-5b9c-9830-314c7e47bb16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757cf839-4729-500e-9a5f-1ad15eb1165b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122527" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3564a5e-caa6-57e8-81ff-e53ef7aa6438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335014} {:answer/id #uuid "9068ddb0-96b2-56c0-a740-39ad426141a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335015} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4b1ecc-3b60-59cd-9632-fb7404a55e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335016} {:answer/id #uuid "50a4b998-ef84-5a1a-8d66-7469d0633aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335017} {:answer/id #uuid "dccb66a0-5394-5321-8281-c000d5cc2308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea4b1ecc-3b60-59cd-9632-fb7404a55e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335016} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ff1cf1-3089-5536-842e-dfc8fb544ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcdbe4d7-f71b-553d-a5f6-fd0190a5304e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122530" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{(\\frac{t}{3})}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da440fb-6395-5d96-be6f-0d568ac52243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335021} {:answer/id #uuid "30e63e57-e949-5b8b-b524-04fae8c50418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335022} {:answer/id #uuid "05910b84-fa7f-5500-b859-b5e7220c2f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335023} {:answer/id #uuid "d8416fd9-86cd-57d8-8bdd-4a485e97458c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335024} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e04fc5-db13-5ab9-b745-fac16cd7e17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05910b84-fa7f-5500-b859-b5e7220c2f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335023} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35222ce-232e-5e45-8b0c-ef3340781282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81d8dc23-7c5c-5870-9fac-a9366f3b9426" :question/math-academy-id "q-122534" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bc97128-ecdc-5ed0-a3b0-055595f5c66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335028} {:answer/id #uuid "a773af4d-38aa-5719-b17f-ae555328b07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335029} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a39d14-7b14-5d8d-9931-a7cd7f38df25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335030} {:answer/id #uuid "1fba58c1-63ad-5137-8a47-bcfc740864cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335031} {:answer/id #uuid "153a753b-8883-5093-8f16-b97f3a9c27f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fba58c1-63ad-5137-8a47-bcfc740864cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335031} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0793448-a8e0-5962-893c-bdaeb63baff7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b923abb3-ac6d-5623-a8b2-5f605625cb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335035} {:answer/id #uuid "332d0350-1b92-5672-aa06-5ad39e3acda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335036} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffce01-8696-538f-b616-db6bd8bda6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335037} {:answer/id #uuid "7a870800-e705-542e-bcfe-a8a6f884160c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(36-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335038} {:answer/id #uuid "7f664c99-e76f-5fb7-aa46-8ccf235296b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f664c99-e76f-5fb7-aa46-8ccf235296b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335039} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1f4e48-9d8f-5833-a66b-531e1c1556b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee3db6e2-712a-58b3-b8b7-1caad2fc22cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122574" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e761f0e-b3b6-5392-81cd-3536b82d3255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335042} {:answer/id #uuid "45e129af-83c6-5331-8d9f-ac5c12915cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335043} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3381e6-724d-556e-8ed4-a881a83c1a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335044} {:answer/id #uuid "84d15ccd-b7db-5362-8632-6db3e250f048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335045} {:answer/id #uuid "8082c768-b9bc-5c36-8027-a41f68ec42b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8082c768-b9bc-5c36-8027-a41f68ec42b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335046} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e31c4b7-2161-5b32-8d41-3b50154a0d77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59534a4f-8afd-50f4-8fbb-8615c0048768" :question/math-academy-id "q-122593" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1f8f55-fef0-588d-8c79-61717b08df58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335049} {:answer/id #uuid "a7494664-fcd7-5e29-99bb-0611f8d8c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335050} {:answer/id #uuid "c73519c0-40fb-5073-8cb6-00aec474c2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335051} {:answer/id #uuid "648ef7c7-e2b4-50cf-9539-2239f26f2b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335052} {:answer/id #uuid "ff510fcb-b967-50f9-af06-cd55532c76ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73519c0-40fb-5073-8cb6-00aec474c2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335051} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e738ad-ab70-5018-9b3e-acfe7304d44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef71fe43-6676-5591-8192-bbf29b95e920" :question/math-academy-id "q-149714" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da29604b-c306-5f14-b00c-f21e120b7224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335056} {:answer/id #uuid "7e845026-062c-57db-a527-5dbd1d0a8cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335057} {:answer/id #uuid "43e6280f-4b51-53c1-9328-bf7ee068ed24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335058} {:answer/id #uuid "03f3d8c0-91bc-5783-8351-f00328ea4fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335059} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0913eb-328a-51d9-a452-2bd0ae217e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03f3d8c0-91bc-5783-8351-f00328ea4fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335059} :answer-field/id #uuid "668caa80-98a9-502f-85ca-39013fb9c0b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d191f591-a7c2-57bc-932c-5a5f3f087d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-149727" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057794/assets/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7be731cd-1503-5c9c-9db7-366a599e493c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335063} {:answer/id #uuid "165d80bc-691d-532b-8f3b-e1ea11737379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335064} {:answer/id #uuid "89d95ab6-b087-5940-93f5-230ae06de0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335065} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5f7276-4e53-5ab7-b64a-73e2c50b7056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335066} {:answer/id #uuid "70f14031-79db-5b5c-ad2e-f2e6a373bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f14031-79db-5b5c-ad2e-f2e6a373bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335067} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbf2235-81e5-5e46-9ee4-bc40647be362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35b5f79b-1789-56ee-87fc-9d9e17bf906f" :question/math-academy-id "q-149745" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
